%!testlib
% What a subinterval keeps alive, run through `xpost -d null` by
% run-ps-test.sh: prints SUCCESS iff every assertion holds.
%
% How this is measured. An explicit reclaim closes the arena up, so the used
% figure vmstatus reports comes back down to where the live storage ends.
% Reclamation is therefore read directly: take the figure, allocate the
% object, drop it, reclaim, and take the figure again. Back to where it
% started means the object was recovered; still up by its size means it was
% not, and something is holding it.
%
% This was written the other way round before the arena could be closed up.
% The figure only ever rose then, so a run that reclaimed everything and one
% that reclaimed nothing reported the same number, and reclamation had to be
% inferred from whether a later allocation of the same shape cost anything.
% The direct reading says the same thing without the second allocation, and
% says it about the object under test rather than about the allocator's
% willingness to reuse a block.

% COLLECTION: every reading here is taken immediately after a reclaim this
% program asks for, so a collection running of its own accord in between
% can only have done work this one would have done anyway. The case at the
% end additionally sets VMThreshold far below the block it allocates, which
% is where a request that exhausts the count meets storage that has just
% been given back.

/used { vmstatus pop exch pop } bind def

% --- a composite nothing refers to is reclaimed and reused ----------------

1 vmreclaim
/mark1 used def
/b 60000 array def
/b null def
1 vmreclaim
used mark1 sub /arraycost exch def
arraycost 1000 lt
    (an unreferenced array is reclaimed and the arena comes back) assert

1 vmreclaim
/mark2 used def
/t 60000 string def
/t null def
1 vmreclaim
used mark2 sub /stringcost exch def
stringcost 1000 lt
    (an unreferenced string is reclaimed and the arena comes back) assert

% --- a subinterval keeps its own contents readable ------------------------
% Whatever the collector does with the rest, the window itself must survive
% and still read the elements it was cut from.

/host 600 array def
0 1 599 { dup host 3 1 roll put } for
/win host 100 42 getinterval def
win length 42 eq (a subinterval reports its own length) assert
win 0 get 100 eq (a subinterval reads the element it starts at) assert
win 41 get 141 eq (a subinterval reads its last element) assert
/host null def
1 vmreclaim
win 0 get 100 eq (a subinterval still reads after its host is dropped) assert
win 41 get 141 eq (a subinterval still reads its last element after that) assert

/shost 600 string def
/swin shost 100 42 getinterval def
swin length 42 eq (a string subinterval reports its own length) assert
/shost null def
1 vmreclaim
swin length 42 eq (a string subinterval survives its host being dropped) assert

% -------------------------------------------------------------------------
% KNOWN DEVIATION (TODO, not asserted): a subinterval holds its whole backing
% composite alive, not just the elements it spans.
% Measured by the method above, at the narrower object width: with a
% 42-element window kept out of a 60000-element array and every other
% reference dropped, a reclaim leaves 480040 bytes below the cursor, against
% 40 when the window is dropped as well. A 42-byte window out of a
% 60000-byte string holds 60056 bytes the same way, against 48. So the
% window is what holds the host, and it holds all of it.
% An Adobe interpreter reclaims all but the window: "the only live reference
% is to a 42-element subinterval near the middle of the original large array;
% an Adobe PostScript interpreter ... will in fact reclaim all of the array
% except those 42 elements, on the next collection."
% The PLRM does not require this, so the retention is not a conformance
% failure; it is recorded because a program that slices a small window out of
% a large buffer and keeps the window keeps the buffer, which in a context
% that serves many jobs reads as a leak. Asserting the present behaviour
% would freeze it, so it is written down for the collector work instead.

% --- reuse does not depend on how often collection is asked for ---------
%
% A request that takes the count of bytes allocated since the last
% collection to zero asks for a collection. That is a statement about
% pacing, not about where this block comes from: a block of the right
% size already on the free list is still the answer. With the threshold
% set below twice the block, the second allocation here is exactly such a
% request.
%
% Restore the threshold afterwards: it is a user parameter, and the cases
% above run at the default.
currentuserparams /VMThreshold get /th0 exch def
100000 setvmthreshold
1 vmreclaim
/mark4 used def
/d 60000 array def
/d null def
1 vmreclaim
used mark4 sub 1000 lt
    (a block is recovered however often collection is asked for) assert
th0 setvmthreshold
1 vmreclaim

verdict
